{"tool_name":"imagej-analyze-skeleton","contents":["neubias"],"fetched_metadata":{"neubias":{"body":[{"format":"basic_html","processed":"<p>This plugin tags all pixel/voxels in a skeleton image and then counts all its junctions, triple and quadruple points and branches, and measures their average and maximum length.</p>\n\n<p>Tags are shown in a new window displaying every tag in a different color. You can find it under [Plugins&gt;Skeleton&gt;Analyze Skeleton (2D/3D)]. See <a href=\"http://imagej.net/Skeletonize3D\">Skeletonize3D</a> for an example of how to produce skeleton images.</p>\n\n<p>The voxels are classified into three different categories depending on their 26 neighbors: - End-point voxels: if they have less than 2 neighbors. - Junction voxels: if they have more than 2 neighbors. - Slab voxels: if they have exactly 2 neighbors.</p>\n\n<p>End-point voxels are displayed in blue, slab voxels in orange and junction voxels in purple.</p>\n\n<p>Notice here that, following this notation, the number of junction voxels can be different from the number of actual junctions since some junction voxels can be neighbors of each other.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Output data type: table result, image of the skeleton</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>","summary":"","value":"<p>This plugin tags all pixel/voxels in a skeleton image and then counts all its junctions, triple and quadruple points and branches, and measures their average and maximum length.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tags are shown in a new window displaying every tag in a different color. You can find it under [Plugins&gt;Skeleton&gt;Analyze Skeleton (2D/3D)]. See <a href=\"http://imagej.net/Skeletonize3D\">Skeletonize3D</a> for an example of how to produce skeleton images.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The voxels are classified into three different categories depending on their 26 neighbors: - End-point voxels: if they have less than 2 neighbors. - Junction voxels: if they have more than 2 neighbors. - Slab voxels: if they have exactly 2 neighbors.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>End-point voxels are displayed in blue, slab voxels in orange and junction voxels in purple.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Notice here that, following this notation, the number of junction voxels can be different from the number of actual junctions since some junction voxels can be neighbors of each other.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Output data type: table result, image of the skeleton</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n"}],"changed":[{"format":"Y-m-d\\TH:i:sP","value":"2023-04-27T12:09:17+00:00"}],"created":[{"format":"Y-m-d\\TH:i:sP","value":"2017-09-12T10:23:39+00:00"}],"default_langcode":[{"value":true}],"attribution_instructions":[],"comments_2023":[],"free_tagging":[],"give_feedback_on_this_soft":[{"cid":0,"comment_count":0,"last_comment_name":null,"last_comment_timestamp":1505211818,"last_comment_uid":160,"status":2}],"has_author":[{"value":"Ignacio Arganda-Carreras orcid.org/0000-0003-0229-5722"}],"has_biological_terms":[{"target_id":4883,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"d7b984d3-0ad1-4e17-b99e-d6baff94d55a","url":"/taxonomy/term/4883"},{"target_id":5000,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"75f8dbb0-175a-4cc8-8340-411183042452","url":"/taxonomy/term/5000"},{"target_id":4557,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"39ff4803-2f42-4bb7-bee7-f42fdb163829","url":"/taxonomy/term/4557"},{"target_id":4398,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"7c4118af-5bb1-4b2a-9648-fd75d30a938a","url":"/taxonomy/term/4398"},{"target_id":4544,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"ff21ffcf-acd8-456a-a737-5d3d14a25b1f","url":"/taxonomy/term/4544"},{"target_id":4489,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"a998789b-5f05-4898-8e20-1d830ae62a2c","url":"/taxonomy/term/4489"}],"has_comparison":[],"has_documentation":[],"has_doi":[],"has_entry_curator":[{"target_id":160,"target_type":"user","target_uuid":"099acd7b-9c05-4af7-85ba-5bbf57caf6e9","url":"/user/160"}],"has_function":[{"target_id":4204,"target_type":"taxonomy_term","target_uuid":"http://edamo